 {"step":23,"text":"== stage ==\nYour insurer action list\n15 items to take to your insurer: 15 Question.\nTake this list to your insurer or agent. Each item says why it matters, what to ask and what to say.\nThis list is general information, not insurance advice, and this tool is not a licensed agent or broker. It is based only on the answers you gave, so it can miss things. Check your own policy documents, and talk to your insurer or agent before you change any coverage.\n[Copy my list] [Print my list]\nYour umbrella and what sits under it\nAn umbrella pays only after the liability limit on the policy underneath has been used up, so each limit below is where the umbrella can start to pay for that policy. Your umbrella insurer sets the minimum it wants underneath.\nUmbrella\nYou were not sure of your umbrella limit\nUmbrella limit: Not sure\nAuto liability\nWe need one more answer to check this\nYou were not sure of your auto liability limit or of what your umbrella asks for, so we cannot compare them.\nYour limit: Not sure\nYour umbrella asks for: Not sure\nHome liability\nWe need one more answer to check this\nYou were not sure of your home liability limit or of what your umbrella asks for, so we cannot compare them.\nYour limit: Not sure\nYour umbrella asks for: Not sure\nMotorcycle liability\nWe need one more answer to check this\nYou were not sure of your motorcycle liability limit or of what your umbrella asks for, so we cannot compare them.\nYour limit: Not sure\nYour umbrella asks for: Not sure\nBoat liability\nWe need one more answer to check this\nYou were not sure of your boat liability limit or of what your umbrella asks for, so we cannot compare them.\nYour limit: Not sure\nYour umbrella asks for: Not sure\nQuestion (15)\nThings to ask your insurer or agent, because the right answer depends on your situation.\nQuestion\nGet your own liability limits and what your umbrella requires\nWhy it matters\nThe Insurance Information Institute describes the usual underlying limits as what most insurers want, so your umbrella insurer may ask for something different, and not being sure of yours is fine. You were not sure about your auto liability limit, your home liability limit, your motorcycle liability limit and your boat liability limit, and about what your umbrella asks for on your auto, home, motorcycle and boat policies, so we cannot tell whether your limits meet what your umbrella asks for. An umbrella is extra liability coverage that starts paying only after the limits underneath are used up.\nWhat to ask\nAsk your agent for the list of required underlying limits on your umbrella (look for a heading like \"required underlying limits\"). Then check the summary page of your auto, home, motorcycle and boat policies, called the declarations page, for your own liability limit and compare the two. If any of your limits is lower than the list asks for, raise it, and ask what the umbrella does while a limit is short.\nSay this\nI want to confirm my own liability limits and get the list of limits my umbrella requires so I can compare them.\nSources\nInsurance Information Institute, Should I purchase an umbrella liability policy? (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/should-i-purchase-an-umbrella-liability-policy> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, Homebuyers Insurance Handbook (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/sites/default/files/docs/pdf/2023_triple-i_homeowners_insurance_handbook.pdf> , last verified 2026-10-10\nQuestion\nCheck that your umbrella is the right size\nWhy it matters\nYou were not sure how big your umbrella is, or how much you could stand to lose in a lawsuit. We need both to tell whether your umbrella is big enough: how big it is, and roughly what a lawsuit could cost you. A simple test from the Insurance Information Institute (Triple-I): do you hold property, investments or savings worth more than your liability limits (the most your auto and home policies will pay if you are legally responsible)? If so, your limits deserve a closer look with your agent. Triple-I also lists owning a pool, renting out property, and having a dog or a teen driver at home as things that can lead to lawsuits.\nWhat to ask\nLook for your umbrella's limit on its declarations page (the summary page of the policy), or ask your agent. Add up your savings, investments and property, and compare the total with your liability limits. Then ask your agent whether your umbrella is big enough for that.\nSay this\nI am not sure my umbrella is the right size for what I could lose, so I want to check it.\nSources\nInsurance Information Institute, Homebuyers Insurance Handbook (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/sites/default/files/docs/pdf/2023_triple-i_homeowners_insurance_handbook.pdf> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, Should I purchase an umbrella liability policy? (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/should-i-purchase-an-umbrella-liability-policy> , last verified 2026-10-10\nQuestion\nAsk whether your umbrella extends uninsured and underinsured motorist coverage\nWhy it matters\nUninsured and underinsured motorist coverage (UM/UIM) is an auto coverage that pays you and your passengers when the driver who hurt you has no insurance, drove off, or has too little to cover your loss. How much you have, and whether it is required, varies by state and by policy, and your state's department of insurance can tell you what applies to you. An umbrella is liability protection, so it responds when you are legally responsible for hurting someone else. UM/UIM is different, because it pays you. Whether your umbrella extends it depends on its own wording. If it does not, the UM/UIM limit on your auto policy is the most that coverage would pay you, and a serious injury could run past it.\nWhat to ask\nAsk your agent, or check your umbrella policy, whether it extends uninsured and underinsured motorist coverage. If it does not, ask whether it can be added and what it would cost.\nSay this\nI want to know whether my umbrella extends uninsured and underinsured motorist coverage, and if not, whether it can.\nSources\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, NAIC Consumer Shopping Tool for Auto Insurance (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/sites/default/files/inline-files/topic_transparency_readability_consumer_auto_tool.pdf> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, Protect yourself against uninsured motorists (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/protect-yourself-against-uninsured-motorists> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, What Does Auto Insurance Cover? (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/article/what-you-should-know-about-auto-insurance-coverage> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, What’s an Umbrella Policy? (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/article/whats-umbrella-policy> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, What is an umbrella liability policy? (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/what-is-an-umbrella-liability-policy> , last verified 2026-10-10\nQuestion\nAsk what your umbrella leaves out\nWhy it matters\nAn umbrella is extra liability protection, which pays when you are legally responsible for hurting someone or damaging their property. It is not a promise to cover everything. Regulators' consumer guidance says it does not pay to repair your own home or car, and lists punitive damages (money a court orders on top of the harm itself, to punish bad conduct) as not covered. Umbrellas typically leave out claims that come from a business. They also often add personal injury coverage for claims like libel and slander (false statements that hurt someone's reputation), but the forms differ by insurer, so check what yours includes and leaves out.\nWhat to ask\nAsk your agent for a plain list of what your umbrella excludes. In particular, ask whether it covers anything that comes from a business or side gig, whether it includes personal injury claims like libel and slander, and what that part leaves out, such as harm done on purpose.\nSay this\nI want to know exactly what my umbrella leaves out, so I know where I would be on my own.\nSources\nInsurance Information Institute, Business Vehicle Insurance (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/publication/insuring-your-business-small-business-owners-guide-to-insurance/specific-coverages/business-vehicle-insurance> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, Insuring Your Home Business (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/insuring-your-home-business> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners (Journal of Insurance Regulation), Social Media as a Factor in Personal Injury Underwriting: Risk, Rate and Regulation (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/sites/default/files/inline-files/JIR-ZA-39-01-EL_0.pdf> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, What’s an Umbrella Policy? (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/article/whats-umbrella-policy> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, What is an umbrella liability policy? (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/what-is-an-umbrella-liability-policy> , last verified 2026-10-10\nQuestion\nFind out whether your policy has uninsured and underinsured motorist coverage\nWhy it matters\nYou weren't sure whether your auto policy includes uninsured and underinsured motorist coverage. Uninsured motorist coverage pays you and your passengers when a driver with no insurance, or a hit-and-run driver, causes the crash. Underinsured motorist coverage steps in when the at-fault driver has insurance but their limits are too low to cover your losses. The Insurance Research Council estimated that about 15% of U.S. drivers, more than one in seven, had no insurance in 2023. Whether this coverage is required, and the minimum amounts, differ from state to state, so your state's department of insurance can tell you the rules where you live.\nWhat to ask\nLook for \"uninsured motorist\" or \"UM/UIM\" on the summary page of your policy, called the declarations page. If you don't see it, ask your insurer whether you have it and at what limit. Also ask whether damage to your car from an uninsured driver is covered separately, because that is usually a different coverage and what is offered varies.\nSay this\nI want to confirm whether my policy includes uninsured and underinsured motorist coverage, and at what limit.\nSources\nInsurance Research Council, One in Three Drivers are Either Uninsured or Underinsured in the U.S, Exposing Themselves and Other Drivers to Significant Financial Risk, New IRC Study Shows (opens in a new tab) <https://insurance-research.org/news/one-three-drivers-are-either-uninsured-or-underinsured-us-exposing-themselves-and-other> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, Uninsured Motorists (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/insurance-topics/uninsured-motorists> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, A Shopping Tool for Auto Insurance (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/sites/default/files/consumer-auto-shopping-tool.pdf> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, Protect yourself against uninsured motorists (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/protect-yourself-against-uninsured-motorists> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, A Consumer's Guide to Auto Insurance (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/sites/default/files/publication-aut-pp-consumer-auto.pdf> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, Background on: Compulsory Auto/Uninsured Motorists (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/background-on-compulsory-auto-uninsured-motorists> , last verified 2026-10-10\nQuestion\nFind out whether your financed car has gap coverage\nWhy it matters\nYou weren't sure whether you have gap coverage. If your newest car is totaled or stolen, a regular claim pays what the car was worth, not what you still owe, and gap coverage pays the difference. It can come from your auto insurer, the dealer or the lender, and it is also called guaranteed asset protection, or GAP.\nWhat to ask\nLook through your loan and sales paperwork for gap coverage, then ask your lender or dealer whether you have it. A dealer or lender generally cannot make you buy gap unless your sales contract explicitly says so, so that contract is the place to look. Ask your auto insurer whether your policy includes it too. If you find you don't have it, ask what adding it would cost, since prices vary widely.\nSay this\nI want to confirm whether gap coverage is in place on my car, and what it would cost to add if it is not.\nSources\nInsurance Information Institute, What is gap insurance? (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/what-is-gap-insurance> , last verified 2026-10-10\nConsumer Financial Protection Bureau, What is Guaranteed Asset Protection (GAP) insurance? (opens in a new tab) <https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/what-is-guaranteed-asset-protection-gap-insurance-en-797/> , last verified 2026-10-10\nConsumer Financial Protection Bureau, Am I required to purchase an extended warranty, Guaranteed Asset Protection (GAP) insurance, or credit insurance from a lender or dealer to get an auto loan? (opens in a new tab) <https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/am-i-required-to-purchase-an-extended-warranty-or-guaranteed-asset-protection-gap-insurance-from-a-lender-or-dealer-to-get-an-auto-loan-en-807/> , last verified 2026-10-10\nQuestion\nFind out whether your policy pays for a rental car\nWhy it matters\nYou weren't sure whether your policy would pay for a rental. Rental reimbursement pays a limited amount per day toward a rental car while your own car is being repaired after a covered claim. It is optional, and policies cap both the daily amount and the number of days. It is different from insurance for a car you rent on a trip. In some states this coverage is tied to your collision and comprehensive coverage instead, so check your own policy.\nWhat to ask\nCheck the summary page of your policy, or ask your insurer, whether you have rental reimbursement and, if so, what the daily and total limits are.\nSay this\nI want to confirm whether my policy pays for a rental while my car is repaired, and what the limits are.\nSources\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, A Shopping Tool for Auto Insurance (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/sites/default/files/consumer-auto-shopping-tool.pdf> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, What Does Auto Insurance Cover? (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/article/what-you-should-know-about-auto-insurance-coverage> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, Does your vehicle have the right protection? Best practices for buying auto insurance (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/article/consumer-insight-does-your-vehicle-have-right-protection-best-practices-buying-auto-insurance> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, A Consumer's Guide to Auto Insurance (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/sites/default/files/publication-aut-pp-consumer-auto.pdf> , last verified 2026-10-10\nQuestion\nFind out if your home policy covers sewer and drain backup\nWhy it matters\nSewer and drain backup is when sewage or water comes back up through your drains or sewer line and damages your floors, walls and belongings. A typical home policy does not cover it, but some policies include it, so the only way to know is to check yours. A flood policy would not fill the gap: the federal flood program pays for sewer backup only when the backup is a direct result of a flood. Where it is missing, the usual fix is an add-on to your home policy, called an endorsement or rider, which most insurers offer for a small extra cost.\nWhat to ask\nAsk your insurer or agent whether your home policy has sewer and drain backup coverage; it may be listed as water backup. If it does, ask for the limit and whether it covers sump pump overflow, which many policies leave out, if you have a sump pump (the pump that keeps a basement dry). If it does not, ask for a quote to add it and what the limit would be.\nSay this\nA typical home policy leaves out sewer and drain backup, so I want to confirm whether mine covers it and at what limit.\nSources\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, Homeowners Insurance (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/insurance-topics/homeowners-insurance> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, Which disasters are covered by homeowners insurance? (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/which-disasters-are-covered-by-homeowners-insurance> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, How to protect your home from water damage (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/how-to-protect-your-home-from-water-damage> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, Protect your house from sewer backups (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/protect-your-house-from-sewer-backups> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, Searching for a Homeowners Insurance Policy? Tips to Get the Most Value (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/article/consumer_insight_searching_homeowners_insurance_policy_tips_get_most_value.htm> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, What is an Insurance Endorsement or Rider? (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/article/consumer-insight-what-insurance-endorsement-or-rider> , last verified 2026-10-10\nFEMA National Flood Insurance Program (FloodSmart), Floodsmart | The National Flood Insurance Program (opens in a new tab) <https://www.floodsmart.gov/> , last verified 2026-10-10\nQuestion\nCheck whether you have a separate flood policy\nWhy it matters\nStandard home policies do not cover flood damage, whether or not your home is in a mapped flood zone, so you need a separate flood policy to be covered. The exclusion is how the policies are written, not a prediction about your home. Flood coverage comes from the federal National Flood Insurance Program (an agent can quote it) or from private insurers, which may offer higher limits or broader coverage. Flood losses are not limited to high-risk areas: about 29 percent of claims to the National Flood Insurance Program from 2014 to 2024 came from outside the areas mapped as high risk. A new federal flood policy usually does not start until 30 days after you buy it, and some private policies have the same wait, so do not wait for a storm warning.\nWhat to ask\nCheck your policy papers or ask your agent whether you have a separate flood policy. If you do not, ask what flood risk they see for your address, then get quotes from the federal program and from private insurers, including what each would cost, how much it would pay for your home and for your belongings, and when it would start. The federal limits can change, so confirm them at floodsmart.gov.\nSay this\nHome policies do not cover flood, so I want to confirm whether I have a separate flood policy and, if not, what one would cost.\nSources\nInsurance Information Institute, Spotlight on: Flood insurance (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/spotlight-on-flood-insurance> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, Flood Insurance (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/consumer/flood-insurance.htm> , last verified 2026-10-10\nFEMA National Flood Insurance Program (FloodSmart), Floodsmart | The National Flood Insurance Program (opens in a new tab) <https://www.floodsmart.gov/> , last verified 2026-10-10\nFEMA, Waiting Period for Activating Flood Policy? (opens in a new tab) <https://www.fema.gov/faq/waiting-period-activating-flood-policy> , last verified 2026-10-10\nFEMA National Flood Insurance Program (FloodSmart), What is My Flood Risk | The National Flood Insurance Program (opens in a new tab) <https://www.floodsmart.gov/flood-zones-and-maps/what-is-my-flood-risk> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, Which disasters are covered by homeowners insurance? (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/which-disasters-are-covered-by-homeowners-insurance> , last verified 2026-10-10\nQuestion\nCheck whether your home policy already covers your child's belongings\nWhy it matters\nWhether your home policy reaches a child's belongings depends on where they live. A student in a dorm who is still part of your household is generally covered by your home policy's off-premises coverage (the part that protects belongings away from home), which means a separate renters policy may be a duplicate. A student who lives off campus is likely not covered, and probably needs their own renters policy. Either way the details vary by insurer. Your policy may pay only up to a set amount for belongings away from home, and your deductible (what you pay first on any claim) still applies.\nWhat to ask\nAsk your home insurer whether your policy covers your child's belongings where they live now, up to how much, and what conditions apply, such as their age and whether they are enrolled in classes. Then compare the answers with what your child's renters policy pays, and ask the renters insurer what it adds beyond your home policy.\nSay this\nI want to know whether my home policy covers my child's belongings where they live, and what a separate renters policy adds.\nSources\nInsurance Information Institute, Protecting your college student from on-campus losses (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/protecting-your-college-student-from-on-campus-losses> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, Do I Need Renters Insurance For College? (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/article/do-i-need-renters-insurance-college> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, Renters Insurance for College Students (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/article/consumer-insight-renters-insurance-college-students> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, Am I Covered? (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/am-i-covered> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, Renters Insurance (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/renters-insurance> , last verified 2026-10-10\nQuestion\nAsk how your auto medical coverage works with your health plan\nWhy it matters\nYou were not sure whether your auto policy pays medical bills for you and your passengers after a crash. That coverage is called medical payments (MedPay) on some policies and personal injury protection (PIP) on others. You can find it on the summary page of your policy, called the declarations page. If you have it, it can overlap with your health plan, which can also pay for crash injuries. How the two work together depends on your state's rules and the exact wording of both policies. Some states require this coverage, and if yours does you may not be able to drop it. Homeowners policies have a similar-sounding add-on, medical payments to others, but it pays for guests hurt at your home, not for your own household, so it is not part of this overlap.\nWhat to ask\nAsk your auto insurer whether your policy includes medical payments or PIP coverage and what it pays for. If it does, ask whether your state requires it, how it works alongside your health plan after a crash, and whether you still need it. Then ask your health insurer how it handles crash injuries when you also have auto medical coverage.\nSay this\nI have health insurance and want to know whether my auto policy also pays medical bills and how the two would work together.\nSources\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, What Does Auto Insurance Cover? (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/article/what-you-should-know-about-auto-insurance-coverage> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, Auto insurance basics—understanding your coverage (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/auto-insurance-basics-understanding-your-coverage> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, Coordination of Benefits Model Regulation (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/sites/default/files/model-law-120.pdf> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, What is covered by standard homeowners insurance? (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/what-is-covered-by-standard-homeowners-insurance> , last verified 2026-10-10\nQuestion\nAsk whether your extra health policies are worth their cost\nWhy it matters\nYou were not sure whether you pay for any extra health policies. These are plans like accident, critical illness, hospital indemnity or cancer insurance that you can buy on top of your main health plan, each tied to one kind of event, like an accident, a hospital stay or a cancer diagnosis. Hospital indemnity and other fixed indemnity policies pay you a set amount of cash, and federal regulators say that kind of coverage is not a substitute for comprehensive health coverage. Accident, cancer and critical illness policies are different products. They may pay you a set amount of cash when a covered event happens or they may work another way, so check how yours pays. You might pay for them through a paycheck deduction at work or on your own. Federal law generally treats these policies as add-ons that fall outside most of the rules for major medical plans, so they sit alongside your main plan, not in place of it.\nWhat to ask\nLook at your pay stub, your benefits summary, and your bank or card statements for lines like accident, critical illness, hospital indemnity or cancer insurance. If you find one, ask which events trigger a payout, whether it pays a set amount or repays specific costs, how much it pays, and what you pay in premiums over a year. If open enrollment is coming up at your job or on the Marketplace, this is a good moment to ask. Your employer sets its own open enrollment dates, so check with them. On HealthCare.gov, open enrollment for 2027 coverage runs November 1 to January 15.\nSay this\nI want to check whether I am paying for any extra health policies before I decide whether they are worth keeping.\nSources\nU.S. Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services, Short-Term, Limited-Duration Insurance and Independent, Noncoordinated Excepted Benefits Coverage (CMS-9904-F) Fact Sheet (opens in a new tab) <https://www.cms.gov/newsroom/fact-sheets/short-term-limited-duration-insurance-and-independent-noncoordinated-excepted-benefits-coverage-cms> , last verified 2026-10-10\nU.S. Department of Labor (Employee Benefits Security Administration), with HHS and Treasury, FAQs about Affordable Care Act Implementation Part 72 (opens in a new tab) <https://www.dol.gov/agencies/ebsa/about-ebsa/our-activities/resource-center/faqs/aca-part-72> , last verified 2026-10-10\nU.S. Federal Trade Commission (Consumer Advice), How to avoid health insurance scams this open enrollment season (opens in a new tab) <https://consumer.ftc.gov/consumer-alerts/2025/12/how-avoid-health-insurance-scams-open-enrollment-season> , last verified 2026-10-10\nHealthCare.gov (U.S. Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services), When can you get health insurance? | HealthCare.gov (opens in a new tab) <https://www.healthcare.gov/quick-guide/dates-and-deadlines/> , last verified 2026-10-10\nHealthCare.gov (U.S. Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services), Open Enrollment Period - Glossary | HealthCare.gov (opens in a new tab) <https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/open-enrollment-period/> , last verified 2026-10-10\nQuestion\nCheck where the towing help on your motorcycle or boat comes from\nWhy it matters\nThe National Association of Insurance Commissioners (NAIC, a group of state insurance regulators) says a car insurance policy does not cover motorcycles, so the towing help on your car policy generally does not reach one. The Insurance Information Institute (Triple-I, an insurance industry research group) lists towing after an accident among the common and optional coverages on a boat policy, so check whether yours already has it. So tow help for your motorcycle or boat may come from its own policy, a club or a card. We did not find anything saying these plans usually repeat each other, so this is a question to ask, not a verdict. Card benefits change often and differ from card to card, so treat this as a prompt to check, not a promise of coverage. Your own card's current benefits guide has the final word.\nWhat to ask\nFirst find out whether your towing plan is on your motorcycle or boat policy: check the summary page of the policy or ask your agent. If it is, ask exactly what it covers. Also ask any auto club or credit card that gives you roadside help whether it reaches your motorcycle or boat at all. If two of them cover the same thing, keep the one you would actually call.\nSay this\nI want to know whether the towing plan on my motorcycle or boat repeats help I already get from another plan.\nSources\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, What Does Auto Insurance Cover? (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/article/what-you-should-know-about-auto-insurance-coverage> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, Boat insurance and safety (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/boat-insurance-and-safety> , last verified 2026-10-10\nQuestion\nCheck whether a card or your home or renters policy already covers your phone\nWhy it matters\nYou told us you pay a monthly fee to insure your phone. Before you keep paying it, check two places that may already cover the same phone. Your homeowners or renters policy generally covers your belongings, including things you carry away from home, up to a limit, and some insurers cap the away-from-home part. Ask whether it covers a phone and what you would pay out of pocket first. If you carry a credit card, check its current benefits guide for cell phone protection before paying separately. Card benefits change often and differ from card to card, so treat this as a prompt to check, not a promise of coverage. Your own card's current benefits guide has the final word.\nWhat to ask\nAsk the issuer of any card you carry whether it protects your phone and what it requires. Ask your home or renters insurer whether a phone is covered and what your deductible would be. Keep the carrier plan until one of them confirms coverage.\nSay this\nI may already have coverage for my phone through a card or my home or renters policy, so I want to confirm that before I keep paying for a separate plan.\nSources\nInsurance Information Institute, What is covered by standard homeowners insurance? (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/what-is-covered-by-standard-homeowners-insurance> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, Renters Insurance (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/renters-insurance> , last verified 2026-10-10\nQuestion\nAsk how your accidental death coverage fits with your life insurance\nWhy it matters\nAccidental death coverage, often called AD&D (accidental death and dismemberment), pays a stated amount only when an accident, or certain kinds of accident, causes a death or a loss. Regular life insurance pays your beneficiaries if you die while the policy is active; a term policy, for example, pays if you die during its term. Read your policy for how it defines an accident and for its exclusions, and check what it counts as a loss. You were not sure what regular life insurance you have. That is the first thing to find out, because whether what accidental death coverage adds is worth its price depends on your total coverage, including any through your job.\nWhat to ask\nAsk your benefits office and your insurance agent what life insurance you have, through work or on your own, and how much each pays. Then ask what your accidental death coverage pays for beyond that, including any non-fatal losses, and whether it is worth its price.\nSay this\nI want to know how much life insurance I have in total before I decide what my accidental death coverage is for.\nSources\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, Glossary of Insurance Terms (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/glossary-insurance-terms> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, Life Insurance (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/consumer/life-insurance.htm> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, Life Insurance Roadmap (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/article/consumer-insight-life-insurance-roadmap> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, Should you buy travel insurance? (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/should-you-buy-travel-insurance> , last verified 2026-10-10\nYour answers stay on this page and are not saved. Nothing about them is counted or sent anywhere. Close or refresh the page and they are gone.\n[Back] [Start over]\n== known ==\nAuto insurance\nHomeowners insurance\nRenters insurance\nUmbrella insurance\nHealth insurance\nMotorcycle insurance\nBoat insurance\nA towing or roadside plan\nPhone insurance\nAccidental death coverage\n== progress ==\n100%"}
